Sorry, I just got sent an
invitation from a good friend.

00:10:32.191 --> 00:10:34.359
He's a 15 year old
entrepreneur who works remotely

00:10:34.359 --> 00:10:35.794
on a daily basis.

00:10:35.794 --> 00:10:37.140
Here to learn, do you have advice.

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:39.186
Do we have advice for a
15 year old entrepreneur

00:10:39.186 --> 00:10:42.132
who wants to work remotely
in their business?

00:10:42.132 --> 00:10:43.317
What advice would you give?

00:10:43.317 --> 00:10:47.122
- Yeah, I think if you want to
work remotely then, I guess,

00:10:47.122 --> 00:10:48.598
first of all you want to ask yourself

00:10:48.598 --> 00:10:50.351
what are you passionate about?

00:10:50.351 --> 00:10:53.456
You know, I know this guy on
my left is passionate about

00:10:53.456 --> 00:10:54.928
helping entrepreneurs.

00:10:54.928 --> 00:10:58.605
So, you know, the channel was
just a vehicle where he can

00:10:58.605 --> 00:11:01.919
serve you, be your mentor,
give you great advice,

00:11:01.919 --> 00:11:03.306
support your greater business.

00:11:03.306 --> 00:11:05.637
So, the first thing is,
you know, as a 15 year old

00:11:05.637 --> 00:11:07.679
there's got to be something you love.

00:11:07.679 --> 00:11:09.981
You know, is it sport,
is it salsa dancing,

00:11:09.981 --> 00:11:13.998
what is it you love that
really you get excited about?

00:11:13.998 --> 00:11:16.951
Because if you get excited
about it you can inspire,

00:11:16.951 --> 00:11:19.489
you know, a movement, a
movement of customers.

00:11:19.489 --> 00:11:21.495
Then, the second thing, I think,

00:11:21.495 --> 00:11:23.416
if you want to work remotely,
you got to think about

00:11:23.416 --> 00:11:25.626
how are you going to deliver that service

00:11:25.626 --> 00:11:27.761
or product to customers.

00:11:27.761 --> 00:11:30.331
And again, I think the
world of social media

00:11:30.331 --> 00:11:32.626
really does amplify that.

00:11:32.626 --> 00:11:34.267
The Internet's changed everything.

00:11:34.267 --> 00:11:37.626
And it's fantastic, it gives
people huge flexibility

00:11:37.626 --> 00:11:38.935
in terms of where they work.

00:11:38.935 --> 00:11:41.068
And as a matter, you know,
we're doing this broadcast

00:11:41.068 --> 00:11:44.586
from Toronto, no fancy
studios, just a laptop.

00:11:44.586 --> 00:11:46.836
Pretty low cost, probably the tech here

00:11:46.836 --> 00:11:48.677
costs less than $1,000.

00:11:48.677 --> 00:11:50.353
So the world has changed.

00:11:50.353 --> 00:11:52.734
This would have been
unimaginable 10 years ago.

00:11:52.734 --> 00:11:55.801
So, I'd say, yeah, absolutely
be passionate about

00:11:55.801 --> 00:11:57.677
something you really love
because that will give you

00:11:57.677 --> 00:11:59.027
your idea for the business.

00:11:59.027 --> 00:12:01.028
And then think about where the customer is

00:12:01.028 --> 00:12:02.011
and how you get there.

00:12:02.011 --> 00:12:04.553
And I'd commend you if
you want to work remotely

00:12:04.553 --> 00:12:07.334
to really look at social
media and the channels,

00:12:07.334 --> 00:12:09.620
whether it's Instagram,
Facebook, or whatever channel,

00:12:09.620 --> 00:12:11.261
where your audience is.

00:12:11.261 --> 00:12:15.027
- Yeah, I would say also
continue to learn and reach out

00:12:15.027 --> 00:12:16.376
and model success.

00:12:16.376 --> 00:12:19.159
I wish that this existed when I was 15.

00:12:19.159 --> 00:12:21.864
I wish I could have asked
Steven Kelly a question

00:12:21.864 --> 00:12:25.018
as a 15 year old entrepreneur
and so I think continue to

00:12:25.018 --> 00:12:28.042
surround yourself with
resources, with people,

00:12:28.042 --> 00:12:29.929
you know, watch YouTube
videos, read books,

00:12:29.929 --> 00:12:32.675
go to Sage Summit if it's in your city,

00:12:32.675 --> 00:12:35.008
continue to try to invest
and learn and model success

00:12:35.008 --> 00:12:38.489
because surrounding yourself
with people who've done

00:12:38.489 --> 00:12:40.939
great things, even if it's
not, maybe there's nobody

00:12:40.939 --> 00:12:44.331
in your family who's done great
things but by reading books

00:12:44.331 --> 00:12:46.781
you get a window into somebody else's mind

00:12:46.781 --> 00:12:49.278
or watching the videos that
we make, you get a window

00:12:49.278 --> 00:12:51.740
into somebody's mind and
by constantly exposing

00:12:51.740 --> 00:12:54.767
yourself to those things,
your perspective will shift,

00:12:54.767 --> 00:12:57.553
your opportunities will open
up and you'll see new ways

00:12:57.553 --> 00:12:59.434
to navigate to help you
build that business.

00:12:59.434 --> 00:13:04.378
And so, Steven's very, like,
tactical, great strategies

00:13:04.378 --> 00:13:07.120
and I would just add
just continue to educate,

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:09.169
continue to learn, continue to reach out.

00:13:09.169 --> 00:13:11.047
Come back, ask more questions, right?

00:13:11.047 --> 00:13:13.991
It's an amazing world we live in right now

00:13:13.991 --> 00:13:15.623
with the opportunity to
reach so many people.

00:13:15.623 --> 00:13:17.266
And I think people are
also willing to help

00:13:17.266 --> 00:13:19.511
and help out young entrepreneurs.

00:13:19.511 --> 00:13:21.848
I just like, I love, when
I see someone say they're

00:13:21.848 --> 00:13:23.283
15 years old, it's like, I love it.

00:13:23.283 --> 00:13:26.273
We've had 13 year olds on, it's great.

00:13:26.273 --> 00:13:28.201
Like, I think a lot of
people want to give back

00:13:28.201 --> 00:13:30.460
and help the next generation as well.

00:13:30.460 --> 00:13:34.518
So, use that, it's an advantage right now,

00:13:34.518 --> 00:13:35.914
and then go take action on it.

00:13:35.914 --> 00:13:39.103
- Yeah, I think, don't
underestimate how much

00:13:39.103 --> 00:13:41.877
people like me, you know,
Evan's obviously loads,

00:13:41.877 --> 00:13:45.731
way younger than I am, but he's been down

00:13:45.731 --> 00:13:47.620
on the mentoring floor
and I remember last year

00:13:47.620 --> 00:13:50.487
I was on the mentoring floor
and I had a 13 year old

00:13:50.487 --> 00:13:54.529
come along to talk to me about
a brilliant app idea that,

00:13:54.529 --> 00:13:58.175
in this case, he'd had and he started off

00:13:58.175 --> 00:14:00.060
and if it was going to be successful,

00:14:00.060 --> 00:14:01.858
he was even kind of
thinking about reducing

00:14:01.858 --> 00:14:05.786
his education and college and
that she's starting a business

00:14:05.786 --> 00:14:06.974
in parallel with that.

00:14:06.974 --> 00:14:11.141
So, I just can't believe how
much people like Evan, me,

00:14:12.372 --> 00:14:15.324
would love to help younger
generation entrepreneurs.

00:14:15.324 --> 00:14:16.759
The other thing, when I kind of grew up,

00:14:16.759 --> 00:14:19.746
I used to kind of think,
wow, I'll go to a conference

00:14:19.746 --> 00:14:22.366
and I'll see someone I
really respect and admire.

00:14:22.366 --> 00:14:24.874
I sort of try and hang around
by the stage door to just,

00:14:24.874 --> 00:14:27.696
you know, doorstep them and say, I just,

00:14:27.696 --> 00:14:29.779
I've got this idea what
do you think of it?

00:14:29.779 --> 00:14:33.300
And again, in your head, you will think,

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:35.669
oh, they won't see me, they
won't have time for me,

00:14:35.669 --> 00:14:38.823
they'll have bodyguards,
they'll have security, you know,

00:14:38.823 --> 00:14:42.792
and now with social media
loads of people DM me.

00:14:42.792 --> 00:14:45.964
I'm sure it's exactly the same for Evan,

00:14:45.964 --> 00:14:48.326
loads of people contact
him through social media.

00:14:48.326 --> 00:14:51.482
And, you know, now Evan's
got to a business where

00:14:51.482 --> 00:14:54.345
it's hugely successful, he
has sort of people helping him

00:14:54.345 --> 00:14:56.233
kind of categorize that
but he still answers

00:14:56.233 --> 00:14:58.522
a lot of that himself.

00:14:58.522 --> 00:15:00.450
And just don't underestimate that people

00:15:00.450 --> 00:15:01.723
are here to help you.

00:15:01.723 --> 00:15:04.922
So if there's voices telling
you in your head that I can't

00:15:04.922 --> 00:15:08.172
access that guy or that lady, so again,

00:15:09.099 --> 00:15:11.759
back to what is your idea,
what are you passionate about?

00:15:11.759 --> 00:15:15.234
Then think of the top three
people who are the experts,

00:15:15.234 --> 00:15:19.085
the sort of, you know,
the rock stars, the gurus,

00:15:19.085 --> 00:15:21.300
and try to reach them
through social media.

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:23.427
And say, look, I'm trying
to think about this.

00:15:23.427 --> 00:15:25.061
I guarantee, if you're 15 years old,

00:15:25.061 --> 00:15:26.039
they'll give you the time,

00:15:26.039 --> 00:15:28.537
they'll give you the
benefit of their advice.

00:15:28.537 --> 00:15:31.529
And from that conversation, that idea,

00:15:31.529 --> 00:15:34.435
and it might refine your
idea, you could have a nugget

00:15:34.435 --> 00:15:35.919
that you can actually start a business.

00:15:35.919 --> 00:15:36.752
- Yeah.

00:15:36.752 --> 00:15:38.503
Just to add on to that,
I would say if they are

00:15:38.503 --> 00:15:41.066
doing an event, just
speaking at a conference

00:15:41.066 --> 00:15:43.556
like Sage Summit, or they're
doing a hangout like this,

00:15:43.556 --> 00:15:44.389
be first.

00:15:45.607 --> 00:15:48.226
A lot of times you're
afraid to go up and be first

00:15:48.226 --> 00:15:49.949
and you want to wait for
the crowd but then it just

00:15:49.949 --> 00:15:51.137
gets too crazy.

00:15:51.137 --> 00:15:54.649
You know, I've done three
different chunks of mentoring time

00:15:54.649 --> 00:15:55.837
here at Sage.

00:15:55.837 --> 00:15:59.039
My first one was right after
the keynote and it was empty

00:15:59.039 --> 00:16:00.914
because everybody was going
to the bathroom and, you know,

00:16:00.914 --> 00:16:04.228
chatting and getting
used to the surroundings.

00:16:04.228 --> 00:16:07.639
And then the other two were
like insane where I'm being

00:16:07.639 --> 00:16:10.294
triple-booked and quadra-booked
and booking on my break

00:16:10.294 --> 00:16:13.331
and talking as I'm walking
into other meetings.

00:16:13.331 --> 00:16:15.333
And that happens, you know.

00:16:15.333 --> 00:16:16.686
If you try to get to a speaker

00:16:16.686 --> 00:16:19.998
after they've done their speech,
they're going to be swamped.

00:16:19.998 --> 00:16:21.068
Get to them first.

00:16:21.068 --> 00:16:22.300
You want to get your question answered

00:16:22.300 --> 00:16:24.588
on a hangout like this,
like, get in there first.

00:16:24.588 --> 00:16:26.432
Get in there sooner so it's noticed.

00:16:26.432 --> 00:16:29.508
So if you're trying to get
on somebody's radar at that

00:16:29.508 --> 00:16:32.535
kind of event or a live
event, just get in there.

00:16:32.535 --> 00:16:34.866
You have your question, this
is what you came here for,

00:16:34.866 --> 00:16:37.194
you want to be around them,
you want to get an answer

00:16:37.194 --> 00:16:38.467
to that question.

00:16:38.467 --> 00:16:41.004
Don't hesitate to just jump
right in and get it answered.

00:16:41.004 --> 00:16:41.837
- So true.

00:16:41.837 --> 00:16:43.337
It's great advice, really great advice.

00:16:43.337 --> 00:16:45.791
And I think conferences
are brilliant, brilliant.

00:16:45.791 --> 00:16:48.613
And if you think about it, when
Evan's done a big conference

00:16:48.613 --> 00:16:52.780
speech or keynoted somewhere,
typically, typically,

00:16:53.720 --> 00:16:56.874
that individual is not
rushing away immediately to go

00:16:56.874 --> 00:16:57.933
and do another keynote.

00:16:57.933 --> 00:16:59.816
Normally they kind of spend, you know,

00:16:59.816 --> 00:17:03.877
we had Michael Hyatt,
keynote here yesterday.

00:17:03.877 --> 00:17:05.434
Amazing entrepreneur.

00:17:05.434 --> 00:17:10.060
Actually, we had Rick,
amazing comedian, a Canadian,

00:17:10.060 --> 00:17:11.248
Rick Mercer.

00:17:11.248 --> 00:17:13.085
And I was blown away.

00:17:13.085 --> 00:17:16.241
He came down here and spent
an hour on the show floor

00:17:16.241 --> 00:17:17.921
and doing selfies and pictures.

00:17:17.921 --> 00:17:20.183
So, again, people are really generous,

00:17:20.183 --> 00:17:21.908
people are generous with their time.

00:17:21.908 --> 00:17:24.414
So be first and grab
them and doorstep them

00:17:24.414 --> 00:17:26.952
and make sure, you know,
you get your idea across

00:17:26.952 --> 00:17:28.108
because you'll probably find,

00:17:28.108 --> 00:17:30.111
especially if you're 15 years old,

00:17:30.111 --> 00:17:31.732
they'll be so proud of
you going up to them

00:17:31.732 --> 00:17:33.577
and having the confidence to do that.

00:17:33.577 --> 00:17:34.885
And they'll spend time with you.

00:17:34.885 --> 00:17:35.968
- Nice, cool.

00:17:36.975 --> 00:17:38.045
Alright, let's keep going.

00:17:38.045 --> 00:17:40.091
Ask your question, guys, in the Comments.

00:17:40.091 --> 00:17:42.262
Paula, you are together now.

00:17:42.262 --> 00:17:43.345
Quadra! Cool.

00:17:44.797 --> 00:17:46.065
Hi from Tokyo.

00:17:46.065 --> 00:17:46.966
Alright.

00:17:46.966 --> 00:17:47.906
Hi from near London.

00:17:47.906 --> 00:17:49.748
People are checking in
from around the world.

00:17:49.748 --> 00:17:53.145
Oh, I just skipped, we're
skipping to the bottom.

00:17:53.145 --> 00:17:54.167
So who are our are top faves?

00:17:54.167 --> 00:17:55.725
Who are your top favorite entrepreneurs?

00:17:55.725 --> 00:17:56.831
I kind of ask you this question on our,

00:17:56.831 --> 00:17:58.468
on our Facebook hangout.

00:17:58.468 --> 00:18:00.026
But who's your favorite entrepreneur?

00:18:00.026 --> 00:18:01.131
Who do you look up to?

00:18:01.131 --> 00:18:03.261
- There's so many, you know.

00:18:03.261 --> 00:18:04.815
- This is from Marnie, by the way, Marnie.

00:18:04.815 --> 00:18:05.648
- Hey, Marnie.

00:18:05.648 --> 00:18:07.481
So, there are so many.

00:18:08.687 --> 00:18:11.642
What I would say in the
spirit of there's no one who's

00:18:11.642 --> 00:18:13.521
the perfect entrepreneur.

00:18:13.521 --> 00:18:14.498
There's lots of people.

00:18:14.498 --> 00:18:17.651
I love entrepreneurs who are very humble,

00:18:17.651 --> 00:18:19.375
who have that sense of
humility to talk about

00:18:19.375 --> 00:18:22.536
their mistakes, I think
that's self-deprivation,

00:18:22.536 --> 00:18:24.046
it's very powerful.

00:18:24.046 --> 00:18:26.385
And therefore, folks, you know, actually,

00:18:26.385 --> 00:18:28.928
take people like Evan,
unbelievable, just very honest,

00:18:28.928 --> 00:18:30.529
very, very humble guy.

00:18:30.529 --> 00:18:35.024
But then the famous ones
include people like, you know,

00:18:35.024 --> 00:18:37.760
Richard Branson from the sort of multi-,

00:18:37.760 --> 00:18:42.311
sort of consumer brand experience
from airlines to music.

00:18:42.311 --> 00:18:46.280
The guys, and we had actually
Richard on the stage last year

00:18:46.280 --> 00:18:49.960
at Sage Summit, and
again, what a humble guy.

00:18:49.960 --> 00:18:52.209
Unbelievable, actually, awe-inspiring.

00:18:52.209 --> 00:18:55.820
And he's, you know, multi-millionaire
but you'd sit across

00:18:55.820 --> 00:18:57.048
and have a cup of coffee with him

00:18:57.048 --> 00:18:59.879
and he's just a regular guy.

00:18:59.879 --> 00:19:02.675
And then people like, in
our industry, technology,

00:19:02.675 --> 00:19:04.597
people like Steve Jobs.

00:19:04.597 --> 00:19:08.361
You could argue, probably,
Steve Jobs wasn't the nicest guy

00:19:08.361 --> 00:19:10.943
in the world but wow, what
a genius, technology genius.

00:19:10.943 --> 00:19:13.959
And he definitely saw where
the puck was going to go

00:19:13.959 --> 00:19:16.709
and he invented whole
different user experience

00:19:16.709 --> 00:19:20.641
for consumers and he had
dreams about integration

00:19:20.641 --> 00:19:23.879
and convergence of different
technologies to give consumers

00:19:23.879 --> 00:19:26.286
something that he thought
would be very practical.

00:19:26.286 --> 00:19:28.703
And I just, yeah, I'm sure
you've seen the movie,

00:19:28.703 --> 00:19:29.929
or read the books.

00:19:29.929 --> 00:19:32.013
And when you look at him
going into that board meeting

00:19:32.013 --> 00:19:34.263
trying to persuade people about the iPhone

00:19:34.263 --> 00:19:37.659
and half the board kind of
weren't there or maybe even

00:19:37.659 --> 00:19:38.971
thought he was crazy.

00:19:38.971 --> 00:19:42.415
But he had conviction,
belief, and you know,

00:19:42.415 --> 00:19:44.952
because of that moment, it
sort of changed the world

00:19:44.952 --> 00:19:47.971
and great companies were
mentioned like Blackberry.

00:19:47.971 --> 00:19:50.231
They're really kind of not the powerhouse

00:19:50.231 --> 00:19:52.106
that they were anymore.

00:19:52.106 --> 00:19:54.553
And what is it, I think
in three years time,

00:19:54.553 --> 00:19:57.348
it seems like the data
says they'll be six billion

00:19:57.348 --> 00:19:59.260
smartphones on the planet.

00:19:59.260 --> 00:20:01.251
And that is 15 years ago technology

00:20:01.251 --> 00:20:02.826
that wasn't even invented.

00:20:02.826 --> 00:20:06.275
So it's those guys, Jobs,
a great inspiration.

00:20:06.275 --> 00:20:07.895
But I think there's a
lot of entrepreneurs.

00:20:07.895 --> 00:20:09.430
You can get little nuggets

00:20:09.430 --> 00:20:11.047
from many different entrepreneurs.

00:20:11.047 --> 00:20:12.121
Who are your favorites?

00:20:12.121 --> 00:20:14.108
- I agree, so I agree 100%.

00:20:14.108 --> 00:20:17.131
My goal is never to be the next Steve Jobs

00:20:17.131 --> 00:20:18.877
or the next anybody and
it's not the advice.

00:20:18.877 --> 00:20:20.955
It's you want to be the
best version of yourself.

00:20:20.955 --> 00:20:22.862
And you can pull from
lots of different people.

00:20:22.862 --> 00:20:24.897
You like this thing about
Steve Jobs and hate all these

00:20:24.897 --> 00:20:26.760
other things, great, like,
forget those and just

00:20:26.760 --> 00:20:28.631
take that one thing.

00:20:28.631 --> 00:20:30.868
And so it's what I try to
do on this channel is pull

00:20:30.868 --> 00:20:32.691
all of these different
famous entrepreneurs

00:20:32.691 --> 00:20:33.770
and successful people.

00:20:33.770 --> 00:20:35.552
And if you learn one thing
from them and that makes you

00:20:35.552 --> 00:20:37.504
a better you, that's the whole point.

00:20:37.504 --> 00:20:41.275
If I had to pick, though, my
favorite famous entrepreneur

00:20:41.275 --> 00:20:44.470
I default to AP Giannini,
who started Bank of America,

00:20:44.470 --> 00:20:47.446
and he was the guy who
bet on the little guy.

00:20:47.446 --> 00:20:49.893
He was the one who insisted
on giving loans to immigrant

00:20:49.893 --> 00:20:52.594
workers when nobody wanted
to give loans to immigrants,

00:20:52.594 --> 00:20:54.418
blue-collar workers.

00:20:54.418 --> 00:20:56.737
He would lend money to
people based off a handshake

00:20:56.737 --> 00:20:58.190
and a look in their eye,

00:20:58.190 --> 00:21:00.512
if you can believe that from a banker.

00:21:00.512 --> 00:21:03.159
He gave money to Walt Disney
when everybody thought he was

00:21:03.159 --> 00:21:07.388
crazy for wanting to make a
full-length cartoon movie.

00:21:07.388 --> 00:21:10.159
So, not a lot of people know his story,

00:21:10.159 --> 00:21:12.637
even inside Bank of America,
you say AP Giannini,

00:21:12.637 --> 00:21:13.470
and it's like, who's that?

00:21:13.470 --> 00:21:15.879
He's your founder, he's
