just figure out where your audience is,

00:31:38.096 --> 00:31:39.676
combine it with what you're good at.

00:31:39.676 --> 00:31:41.665
You know, Steven started
his new vlog series,

00:31:41.665 --> 00:31:42.945
it can do well.

00:31:42.945 --> 00:31:46.386
Most CEOs, I've met a lot
of them, are super boring

00:31:46.386 --> 00:31:50.003
and would not have a great personal vlog.

00:31:50.003 --> 00:31:52.581
And so maybe that's not the
best path for them, right?

00:31:52.581 --> 00:31:54.279
Even though the audience is on YouTube,

00:31:54.279 --> 00:31:56.853
so you've got to combine
those two things together.

00:31:56.853 --> 00:31:58.901
I think entrepreneurs try to do everything

00:31:58.901 --> 00:32:00.622
and then you do nothing well.

00:32:00.622 --> 00:32:01.997
You try to do YouTube
and Twitter and LinkedIn

00:32:01.997 --> 00:32:04.272
and Medium and Facebook and
Instagram and everything

00:32:04.272 --> 00:32:06.269
and then everything sucks.

00:32:06.269 --> 00:32:08.501
So, pick the platform
that has your audience

00:32:08.501 --> 00:32:12.771
and combines your natural talent
and then make that awesome!

00:32:12.771 --> 00:32:15.682
So that if I watch your YouTube
video or I see your Tweet,

00:32:15.682 --> 00:32:18.001
I'm going to want to consume
it again and share it

00:32:18.001 --> 00:32:19.205
with my friends.

00:32:19.205 --> 00:32:21.276
Making content that you're
proud of it way more important

00:32:21.276 --> 00:32:25.263
than making decent
content across everything.

00:32:25.263 --> 00:32:27.756
- Yeah, actually, that's
such an important, Evan,

00:32:27.756 --> 00:32:32.212
and any advice you'd give in
terms of the different choices

00:32:32.212 --> 00:32:33.876
between the social channels?

00:32:33.876 --> 00:32:36.942
You know, because there's a
lot of factors in terms of

00:32:36.942 --> 00:32:39.840
whether it's content, whether
it's a really high quality

00:32:39.840 --> 00:32:42.408
photograph pic, you know, there's
a lot of different things.

00:32:42.408 --> 00:32:44.017
Any advice you'd give?

00:32:44.017 --> 00:32:46.955
- Well, if you look at like
Twitter, YouTube, Facebook,

00:32:46.955 --> 00:32:48.456
they have all audiences.

00:32:48.456 --> 00:32:49.863
They're so big that they cover everything.

00:32:49.863 --> 00:32:50.896
Instagram, too.

00:32:50.896 --> 00:32:51.891
You can go niche.

00:32:51.891 --> 00:32:53.959
Like, LinkedIn is going to be
more for the business crowd

00:32:53.959 --> 00:32:55.787
and, you know, Pinterest
hits a certain crowd,

00:32:55.787 --> 00:32:58.486
and SnapChat skews younger
but it's coming up older.

00:32:58.486 --> 00:33:00.567
So, you can be a little
strategic about that.

00:33:00.567 --> 00:33:01.901
But, yeah, leverage your strengths.

00:33:01.901 --> 00:33:05.047
If you're a great photographer, you know,

00:33:05.047 --> 00:33:07.042
you take beautiful pictures,

00:33:07.042 --> 00:33:09.327
you can turn that into a YouTube video.

00:33:09.327 --> 00:33:12.145
But it's still pictures,
it's not as great as if you

00:33:12.145 --> 00:33:15.999
turn it into Instagram
photos or Facebook photos.

00:33:15.999 --> 00:33:18.237
Or partner up with a motivational speaker

00:33:18.237 --> 00:33:22.429
and use their quotes to be on
top of your pictures, right?

00:33:22.429 --> 00:33:24.457
So, leverage the thing
that you're really good at

00:33:24.457 --> 00:33:28.265
with where you're audience
is and just start with one.

00:33:28.265 --> 00:33:29.098
- Yeah.

00:33:29.098 --> 00:33:30.126
- I would get all the
domains of all of them.

00:33:30.126 --> 00:33:31.368
Like, get the handles.

00:33:31.368 --> 00:33:32.994
So, if a new thing opened up,

00:33:32.994 --> 00:33:37.558
I would get the Evan Carmichael
love, you know, SuTube,

00:33:37.558 --> 00:33:40.746
just to have it and then
I'd figure out later

00:33:40.746 --> 00:33:42.035
if I'm actually going to use it or not

00:33:42.035 --> 00:33:43.402
so nobody takes your name.

00:33:43.402 --> 00:33:46.721
- Yeah, actually, in some
respects, what Evan said

00:33:46.721 --> 00:33:50.044
has huge impact on me,
said start with one.

00:33:50.044 --> 00:33:54.211
I'd also, I'd encourage
you to start with one idea.

00:33:55.563 --> 00:33:59.001
Don't try and do too many things
because you kind of become

00:33:59.001 --> 00:34:01.074
all things to all people
and you're good at nothing.

00:34:01.074 --> 00:34:03.319
So it's the same with social media.

00:34:03.319 --> 00:34:05.145
Pick one channel.

00:34:05.145 --> 00:34:07.177
Same with kind of product or service,

00:34:07.177 --> 00:34:08.752
just pick one to start with.

00:34:08.752 --> 00:34:11.935
And then, you know, we call it
a sort of beachhead strategy.

00:34:11.935 --> 00:34:14.417
You own one beachhead,
you do really well there,

00:34:14.417 --> 00:34:16.118
then you think about the
next one that's related

00:34:16.118 --> 00:34:18.233
and then you do the next
one and multi next one.

00:34:18.233 --> 00:34:20.007
And that's how you kind of ultimately

00:34:20.007 --> 00:34:22.498
scale a great business.

00:34:22.498 --> 00:34:23.368
- I love it.

00:34:23.368 --> 00:34:24.945
He's writing back to say
once again thanks for

00:34:24.945 --> 00:34:27.020
the awesome content and motivation.

00:34:27.020 --> 00:34:28.179
So that's great.

00:34:28.179 --> 00:34:29.012
Glad we can help.

00:34:29.012 --> 00:34:29.929
Next, Zack.

00:34:31.371 --> 00:34:33.939
Does a positive outlook
play a part in quelling

00:34:33.939 --> 00:34:37.339
some young aspiring
entrepreneur's anxiety.

00:34:37.339 --> 00:34:39.706
So role of positive outlook

00:34:39.706 --> 00:34:42.439
on quelling anxiety for entrepreneurs.

00:34:42.439 --> 00:34:43.556
- Yeah, I think that's so good.

00:34:43.556 --> 00:34:48.029
We better start with the
man who invented #belief.

00:34:48.029 --> 00:34:49.942
This is him on my left.

00:34:49.942 --> 00:34:50.775
It's Evan.

00:34:50.775 --> 00:34:51.805
So important.

00:34:51.805 --> 00:34:54.749
- Yeah, I mean, just like I
started off the talk saying

00:34:54.749 --> 00:34:57.947
I think the mindset is super
important, I think, you know,

00:34:57.947 --> 00:35:01.614
10% of your life is what happens to you

00:35:02.507 --> 00:35:04.412
and 90% is how you react to it

00:35:04.412 --> 00:35:05.866
and how you choose to look at it.

00:35:05.866 --> 00:35:09.066
You know, is this, it's the
difference between is this,

00:35:09.066 --> 00:35:12.798
am I having a bad day or
am I having a growth day?

00:35:12.798 --> 00:35:13.631
Right?

00:35:13.631 --> 00:35:15.120
This is a me growing day.

00:35:15.120 --> 00:35:17.947
There's a concept of would you
know the difference between

00:35:17.947 --> 00:35:20.504
being buried and being planted?

00:35:20.504 --> 00:35:22.400
Because when you're underground
all you see is darkness

00:35:22.400 --> 00:35:25.666
around you and maybe you're
just being planted to grow

00:35:25.666 --> 00:35:29.004
stronger to come up and create
this amazing thing versus

00:35:29.004 --> 00:35:31.784
I'm buried, I'm suffocated,
I can't do anything.

00:35:31.784 --> 00:35:34.686
If you look at all the people
who've had massive success,

00:35:34.686 --> 00:35:37.427
it's not inherited wealth,
but genuine massive success

00:35:37.427 --> 00:35:40.753
that they created on their own,
it doesn't come from people

00:35:40.753 --> 00:35:43.836
who had super wealthy parents, right?

00:35:44.777 --> 00:35:48.922
People who grew up with massive
safety nets and trust funds.

00:35:48.922 --> 00:35:52.194
It's the people who had nothing growing up

00:35:52.194 --> 00:35:55.257
and they turned it into something amazing.

00:35:55.257 --> 00:35:58.568
And I think a big challenge
is if you don't have anything

00:35:58.568 --> 00:36:02.504
right now, the people around
you can be telling you

00:36:02.504 --> 00:36:03.337
that you don't have anything,

00:36:03.337 --> 00:36:04.823
that you won't amount to anything,

00:36:04.823 --> 00:36:07.892
that you don't have the
education or background or money

00:36:07.892 --> 00:36:11.336
or anything and so
changing your environment,

00:36:11.336 --> 00:36:13.200
whether it's watching videos
like this or reading books,

00:36:13.200 --> 00:36:14.554
all the stuff I talked about,

00:36:14.554 --> 00:36:16.053
whatever it is that gets you excited,

00:36:16.053 --> 00:36:18.709
because we've all had
these moments of boldness

00:36:18.709 --> 00:36:22.481
and excitement and velocity,
one of Steven's favorite words,

00:36:22.481 --> 00:36:23.931
we've all had these moments
where we want to just go out

00:36:23.931 --> 00:36:25.303
and charge and do these amazing things.

00:36:25.303 --> 00:36:26.420
Like, what led to that?

00:36:26.420 --> 00:36:29.453
And then how do you make that
part of your daily routine

00:36:29.453 --> 00:36:32.069
instead of happening to come
across it at five o'clock

00:36:32.069 --> 00:36:35.967
on a Tuesday afternoon,
so that you're applying it

00:36:35.967 --> 00:36:37.211
on a regular basis.

00:36:37.211 --> 00:36:39.246
I think the mindset, the
environment is super,

00:36:39.246 --> 00:36:43.069
super important, much more than
whatever resources you have.

00:36:43.069 --> 00:36:47.547
- Yeah, I think it's probably
one of the most important

00:36:47.547 --> 00:36:49.750
aspects of a successful entrepreneur.

00:36:49.750 --> 00:36:51.652
And the thing we celebrate is

00:36:51.652 --> 00:36:53.269
we've all got different personalities.

00:36:53.269 --> 00:36:56.466
You know, we're probably,
ironically, introverts.

00:36:56.466 --> 00:37:00.816
And yet, you know, sometimes
that has sort of naturally

00:37:00.816 --> 00:37:02.929
made us quite anxious about stuff.

00:37:02.929 --> 00:37:07.483
But, what I would say is there's
just such strong evidence

00:37:07.483 --> 00:37:11.650
that positivity and optimism
is actually not only inspiring

00:37:13.000 --> 00:37:16.555
but it's also quite
contagious, it's infectious.

00:37:16.555 --> 00:37:19.883
So, and you're customers will detect this.

00:37:19.883 --> 00:37:21.705
And it's tough to fake.

00:37:21.705 --> 00:37:25.226
So authenticity, we've talked
so much about authenticity,

00:37:25.226 --> 00:37:28.635
it's such a powerful, compelling quality.

00:37:28.635 --> 00:37:31.706
But if you're going to hire
like someone to come and work

00:37:31.706 --> 00:37:34.275
with you, you've got to be
positive about your dream

00:37:34.275 --> 00:37:36.800
and your vision, your idea.

00:37:36.800 --> 00:37:39.917
So, and there's just
starting to be a load of like

00:37:39.917 --> 00:37:42.239
sort of behavioral psychology
written around this

00:37:42.239 --> 00:37:44.270
and sort of nudging behaviors.

00:37:44.270 --> 00:37:48.824
But I think you waking up
everyday and having conviction,

00:37:48.824 --> 00:37:51.771
having belief, and
expressing that in optimism

00:37:51.771 --> 00:37:54.255
and positivity about how your product's

00:37:54.255 --> 00:37:56.793
going to change the world
in its own little way,

00:37:56.793 --> 00:37:58.135
is very compelling.

00:37:58.135 --> 00:38:00.579
And I think the other
thing, just practically,

00:38:00.579 --> 00:38:04.184
because you can't be always
sort of on and always positive,

00:38:04.184 --> 00:38:06.630
so sometimes we all
kind of have a bad day,

00:38:06.630 --> 00:38:08.208
whenever that is.

00:38:08.208 --> 00:38:10.363
And if that's the case,
maybe, I don't know,

00:38:10.363 --> 00:38:11.808
you don't want to do
sort of front line work,

00:38:11.808 --> 00:38:13.712
you want to do stuff about thinking.

00:38:13.712 --> 00:38:14.789
So, if you're not having a great day,

00:38:14.789 --> 00:38:17.365
do some thoughtful work, do
some stuff about, I don't know,

00:38:17.365 --> 00:38:18.933
product development, whatever.

00:38:18.933 --> 00:38:21.314
But if you're having, you're in the zone,

00:38:21.314 --> 00:38:22.681
you're having a good day, you're positive,

00:38:22.681 --> 00:38:25.661
get out there, talk to
customers, talk to media,

00:38:25.661 --> 00:38:28.692
post stuff on social media.

00:38:28.692 --> 00:38:31.386
So play to your strengths
in terms of how you are

00:38:31.386 --> 00:38:35.004
because none of us have like
a consistent sort of mood

00:38:35.004 --> 00:38:37.451
that just always operates
at the same level.

00:38:37.451 --> 00:38:41.020
But I would, I would
absolutely always be encouraged

00:38:41.020 --> 00:38:43.515
by all the entrepreneurs I've met with.

00:38:43.515 --> 00:38:45.918
When they're in the zone,
they're unbelievably positive.

00:38:45.918 --> 00:38:50.066
Because what they're doing
is really persuading us all

00:38:50.066 --> 00:38:52.479
there's a better way,
there's a better world,

00:38:52.479 --> 00:38:53.477
there's a better product,

00:38:53.477 --> 00:38:55.671
there's a different way to do stuff,

00:38:55.671 --> 00:38:57.708
there's a different way to crack the code.

00:38:57.708 --> 00:39:01.153
And all of us get compelled
to follow in that vision

00:39:01.153 --> 00:39:02.357
then it creates a movement.

00:39:02.357 --> 00:39:04.927
And that's how you scale great companies.

00:39:04.927 --> 00:39:05.927
- I like it.

00:39:07.322 --> 00:39:10.101
Vali's asking how do
you transform a bad day

00:39:10.101 --> 00:39:12.268
into a bad minute or hour?

00:39:13.301 --> 00:39:17.035
- Actually, a great way, so if you are,

00:39:17.035 --> 00:39:19.110
you're not in the zone, whatever.

00:39:19.110 --> 00:39:22.010
You could be depressed, you
could just, just had it,

00:39:22.010 --> 00:39:24.875
something happens which
kind knocks you off track.

00:39:24.875 --> 00:39:28.613
What you want to do is choo,
actually sort of cauterize that

00:39:28.613 --> 00:39:30.442
and just move on.

00:39:30.442 --> 00:39:32.591
It could be loads of
different things, you could,

00:39:32.591 --> 00:39:33.713
and it depends on what works for you.

00:39:33.713 --> 00:39:36.207
You could go for a walk, go for a run.

00:39:36.207 --> 00:39:39.057
I tend to, you know,
if I'm, I'm certainly,

00:39:39.057 --> 00:39:40.879
the way that I beat jet
lag and stuff like that

00:39:40.879 --> 00:39:43.201
is just go running, gets me feeling good,

00:39:43.201 --> 00:39:46.072
gets the old endorphins
going, all that sort of stuff.

00:39:46.072 --> 00:39:48.979
Or you could just go and go to a mentor,

00:39:48.979 --> 00:39:51.421
you could go and have
a coffee with someone,

00:39:51.421 --> 00:39:53.784
you could do breathing exercises,

00:39:53.784 --> 00:39:54.823
you could do mindfulness,

00:39:54.823 --> 00:39:56.734
you could do whatever
works for you, really.

00:39:56.734 --> 00:39:59.801
I think it's really
important that you are always

00:39:59.801 --> 00:40:03.943
your unique personality and you
find just what works for you

00:40:03.943 --> 00:40:07.879
and allows you to kind of
recalibrate your optimism

00:40:07.879 --> 00:40:09.751
and positivity and come back.

00:40:09.751 --> 00:40:11.238
And if you can't, you know, its great,

00:40:11.238 --> 00:40:14.179
instead of having a bad
day, just have a bad minute.

00:40:14.179 --> 00:40:16.089
Wow, that's powerful, really is powerful.

00:40:16.089 --> 00:40:17.872
And then bounce back.

00:40:17.872 --> 00:40:19.283
- A couple of things that help me.

00:40:19.283 --> 00:40:21.194
One, try to solve the problem.

00:40:21.194 --> 00:40:22.770
Like, if it's a problem that's happening,

00:40:22.770 --> 00:40:24.217
there's this crisis in my business,

00:40:24.217 --> 00:40:25.544
or there's something I need to solve,

00:40:25.544 --> 00:40:27.411
okay, go out and solve the problem.

00:40:27.411 --> 00:40:29.483
Like, do it even if it's scary
because the more you just

00:40:29.483 --> 00:40:31.715
let it sit, the problem's
not just going to go away

00:40:31.715 --> 00:40:32.919
on its own.

00:40:32.919 --> 00:40:35.116
So go out and solve it.

00:40:35.116 --> 00:40:37.315
If it's the kind of
thing that I can't solve

00:40:37.315 --> 00:40:39.143
or I'm frustrated for a different reason,

00:40:39.143 --> 00:40:41.757
I like the line of
turning your expectations

00:40:41.757 --> 00:40:43.332
into appreciation.

00:40:43.332 --> 00:40:46.272
So, I'm sitting in traffic, you know,

00:40:46.272 --> 00:40:48.505
or I'm waiting for the
elevator and it's not working

00:40:48.505 --> 00:40:51.065
or something happens that's
just beyond my control,

00:40:51.065 --> 00:40:55.175
just turning your expectations
into appreciation,

00:40:55.175 --> 00:40:58.541
gratitude, like, I'm in a
car, like, this is great.

00:40:58.541 --> 00:41:00.990
I can drive and like imagine
if it was 100 years ago,

00:41:00.990 --> 00:41:03.326
I'd be on a horse, you know.

00:41:03.326 --> 00:41:06.893
And I get to talk to people
like you guys over the internet

00:41:06.893 --> 00:41:09.917
everyday and it wouldn't
have been able to happen

00:41:09.917 --> 00:41:10.786
100 years ago, right?

00:41:10.786 --> 00:41:13.401
Just that kind of stuff just
resets and makes me realize

00:41:13.401 --> 00:41:15.387
that most of these things
that I'm upset about

00:41:15.387 --> 00:41:18.286
don't actually really matter
and to get back on the horse

00:41:18.286 --> 00:41:20.817
and go do the thing that I care about.

00:41:20.817 --> 00:41:22.635
So solve the problem if there is a problem

00:41:22.635 --> 00:41:24.839
you can solve immediately, don't delay.

00:41:24.839 --> 00:41:27.612
And two, trade your
expectations for appreciation.

00:41:27.612 --> 00:41:29.361
- Yeah, actually, the other
thing, just looking back

00:41:29.361 --> 00:41:33.339
on my career, you know, you
do worry about stuff at times.

00:41:33.339 --> 00:41:36.532
But when I look back on it
with the benefit of hindsight,

00:41:36.532 --> 00:41:40.218
there's never been anything
that we didn't overcome.

00:41:40.218 --> 00:41:42.704
And I think there's a great
quality around perseverance.

00:41:42.704 --> 00:41:44.397
And you just got to give yourself context.

00:41:44.397 --> 00:41:46.102
You've got to step back from the moment,

00:41:46.102 --> 00:41:48.461
sort of be your own shadow,
and then watch yourself

00:41:48.461 --> 00:41:49.619
in the moment.

00:41:49.619 --> 00:41:51.228
And sometimes when you
do that you think, wow,

00:41:51.228 --> 00:41:53.420
I'm being pretty ridiculous,
I'm getting worried about stuff

00:41:53.420 --> 00:41:54.664
I don't need to worry about.

00:41:54.664 --> 00:41:56.609
And there's kind of a few
rules for entrepreneurs,

00:41:56.609 --> 00:41:58.058
rules of life, really.

00:41:58.058 --> 00:42:00.588
You should only worry
about what you can change.

00:42:00.588 --> 00:42:02.993
No point worrying about
stuff you can't, you know,

00:42:02.993 --> 00:42:05.196
if you're worried about, I
don't know, the US president,

00:42:05.196 --> 00:42:07.567
you're worried about
stuff, what can you do?

00:42:07.567 --> 00:42:08.688
Honestly, what can you do about it?

00:42:08.688 --> 00:42:11.297
Let's worry about stuff we
can change, your customers,

00:42:11.297 --> 00:42:14.528
the product, the service,
the channels, you know,

00:42:14.528 --> 00:42:17.304
those things you can really spend time on.

00:42:17.304 --> 00:42:19.464
And just become very pragmatic.

00:42:19.464 --> 00:42:21.336
But what I would say as well, you know,

00:42:21.336 --> 00:42:25.979
starting a business takes
the guts, the perseverance,

00:42:25.979 --> 00:42:28.832
just to go cross the line
and take the first step.

00:42:28.832 --> 00:42:32.230
But then a lot of it really is
about continued perseverance,

00:42:32.230 --> 00:42:36.085
tenacity, grit, and all those
sort of qualities come out

00:42:36.085 --> 00:42:40.650
to the fore in terms of just
the consistency of execution

00:42:40.650 --> 00:42:43.386
over the longterm to be
proud of what you created.

00:42:43.386 --> 00:42:46.202
So, I think a lot of it
is definitely, you know,

00:42:46.202 --> 00:42:49.596
in your hands and we would
definitely encourage you

00:42:49.596 --> 00:42:52.914
in that vein to be really
positive, really optimistic.

00:42:52.914 --> 00:42:55.616
And when you are having a
bad day just allow yourself

00:42:55.616 --> 00:42:58.727
to step back, just be
objective, and say, you know,

00:42:58.727 --> 00:43:00.552
back to Evan's point, what is the problem

00:43:00.552 --> 00:43:03.044
we're trying to solve
here and how do we fix it?

00:43:03.044 --> 00:43:06.363
And then you're creative,
you're smart guys,

00:43:06.363 --> 00:43:08.606
you'll find a way.

00:43:08.606 --> 00:43:10.390
- I like it.

00:43:10.390 --> 00:43:11.922
Daniel's writing in.

00:43:11.922 --> 00:43:14.607
How do you balance real
life, kids, bill paying,

00:43:14.607 --> 00:43:17.424
with investing in your ideas when you have

00:43:17.424 --> 00:43:19.257
more month than money?

00:43:20.533 --> 00:43:22.280
Not sure what that last part
is but basically the idea

00:43:22.280 --> 00:43:24.644
of being able to balance
your life, kids, bill paying,

00:43:24.644 --> 00:43:26.427
with trying to start a business.

00:43:26.427 --> 00:43:28.001
- I think that's a tough one.

00:43:28.001 --> 00:43:32.168
And I would say, you know,
probably this a where I question

00:43:33.305 --> 00:43:37.817
some of the sort of media
portrayal of entrepreneurs

00:43:37.817 --> 00:43:40.385
and sometimes entrepreneurs
are painted as people

00:43:40.385 --> 00:43:44.644
who have everything and never
have to make compromises.

00:43:44.644 --> 00:43:47.584
I don't know those entrepreneurs
because I, you know,

00:43:47.584 --> 00:43:50.239
for Evan, maybe talk, Evan's got kids,

00:43:50.239 --> 00:43:52.480
there's a lot of juggling you have to do.

00:43:52.480 --> 00:43:54.802
We sort of have a belief,
I absolutely love life,

00:43:54.802 --> 00:43:57.993
I love being an entrepreneur
and I say in Sage

00:43:57.993 --> 00:44:00.185
to my colleagues, you
know, think of us as like

00:44:00.185 --> 00:44:02.175
the most excited start-up in the world.

00:44:02.175 --> 00:44:05.826
But in a business, I think
you do have big dreams.

00:44:05.826 --> 00:44:08.765
Across your life, the sadness,
the only sadness about life

00:44:08.765 --> 00:44:11.621
is you just get one, one life.

00:44:11.621 --> 00:44:13.154
And I kind of think for people like Evan,

00:44:13.154 --> 00:44:15.752
one life is never enough
because we've just got so much

00:44:15.752 --> 00:44:17.867
to do with our one life.

00:44:17.867 --> 00:44:19.066
But what does that mean?

00:44:19.066 --> 00:44:23.087
What it means to me is that
in terms of the context

00:44:23.087 --> 00:44:26.733
of balancing things, I think, you know,

00:44:26.733 --> 00:44:29.053
the reality is you've got one
shot, you've got one life,

00:44:29.053 --> 00:44:31.581
you've probably got
loads of business ideas

00:44:31.581 --> 00:44:33.622
and you have to juggle,
you have to make compromise

00:44:33.622 --> 00:44:36.815
if you're having kids, you
have to, what I would advise,

00:44:36.815 --> 00:44:39.640
honestly, I think it's
tough to have it all.

00:44:39.640 --> 00:44:43.831
And, you know, have a
career and work crazy hours

00:44:43.831 --> 00:44:46.653
and balance your life and
keep all the balls juggling

00:44:46.653 --> 00:44:48.774
in the air, it's tough because
you have to compromise.

00:44:48.774 --> 00:44:52.145
But what I would say, and this
would be advice that I would

00:44:52.145 --> 00:44:56.249
give to my younger self,
just go into every situation

00:44:56.249 --> 00:44:58.114
with your eyes wide open.

00:44:58.114 --> 00:45:00.103
Always have the discussion,
whether it's with kids

00:45:00.103 --> 00:45:04.050
or partners or whoever it is,
to be as honest as you can

00:45:04.050 --> 00:45:05.050
at that point in time.

00:45:05.050 --> 00:45:08.738
What you tend to find, again
this sort of with the benefit

00:45:08.738 --> 00:45:13.212
of hindsight, is you can never
really predict the future.

00:45:13.212 --> 00:45:16.731
The downside of me being
an optimist, in some ways,

00:45:16.731 --> 00:45:19.010
as an entrepreneur is
sometimes I say, well,

00:45:19.010 --> 00:45:21.164
we're going to have to double-down
for the next, I don't know,

00:45:21.164 --> 00:45:23.313
say 30 days, 90 days.

00:45:23.313 --> 00:45:26.010
My experience tells me
those 30 days always become

00:45:26.010 --> 00:45:27.710
90 days or 180 days.

00:45:27.710 --> 00:45:31.410
It's always taken me longer
than I've anticipated.

00:45:31.410 --> 00:45:35.313
So my sort of, I guess,
answer to you question is,

00:45:35.313 --> 00:45:36.765
I think you do have to compromise,

00:45:36.765 --> 00:45:39.252
you have to juggle the balls.

00:45:39.252 --> 00:45:42.161
But I think with that comes
a responsibility to be honest

00:45:42.161 --> 00:45:45.641
to yourself and honest with
the people you care about.

00:45:45.641 --> 00:45:49.875
And try and just be, you know,
just have that conversation

00:45:49.875 --> 00:45:52.614
so you go in with your eyes wide open.

00:45:52.614 --> 00:45:55.349
But also, just to
acknowledge that actually

00:45:55.349 --> 00:45:56.958
things might change over time.

00:45:56.958 --> 00:45:59.898
If your business really picks
up and it's going gangbusters

00:45:59.898 --> 00:46:01.181
it might take more time.

00:46:01.181 --> 00:46:03.836
And therefore you're going to
have to double down a bit longer

00:46:03.836 --> 00:46:06.364
so you kind of need a
reset and a checkpoint

00:46:06.364 --> 00:46:08.641
down that journey.

00:46:08.641 --> 00:46:10.587
And again, worth having
the honest conversation

00:46:10.587 --> 00:46:12.662
and just making sure all
the people you care about

00:46:12.662 --> 00:46:15.060
are on board and all committed

00:46:15.060 --> 00:46:18.413
and have that kind of shared meaning.

00:46:18.413 --> 00:46:20.441
- Yeah, I'm reminded of Steve Wozniak,

00:46:20.441 --> 00:46:22.713
whose advice for entrepreneurs,

00:46:22.713 --> 00:46:25.995
he co-founded Apple with Steve
Jobs, would say don't focus

00:46:25.995 --> 00:46:28.230
on what you don't have,
rather, focus on what can do

00:46:28.230 --> 00:46:30.054
with what you do have.

00:46:30.054 --> 00:46:32.343
And I think even just that mindset shift

00:46:32.343 --> 00:46:35.549
is a huge difference because
we tend to focus on all

00:46:35.549 --> 00:46:37.198
the things we don't have,
the money we don't have,

00:46:37.198 --> 00:46:38.856
the time we don't have, the
resources we don't have,

00:46:38.856 --> 00:46:40.927
the connections we don't have,
the education we don't have,

00:46:40.927 --> 00:46:43.248
and if that's the world you live in,

00:46:43.248 --> 00:46:45.148
you're never going to build anything.

00:46:45.148 --> 00:46:47.058
You can't just focus on
what you don't have because

00:46:47.058 --> 00:46:48.919
you're operating in a world of scarcity.

00:46:48.919 --> 00:46:53.740
Where if you focus on, listen,
I have half an hour a night,

00:46:53.740 --> 00:46:54.818
that's what I have.

00:46:54.818 --> 00:46:58.259
Great, like, use that half an
hour to build your business,

00:46:58.259 --> 00:46:59.583
every single day.

00:46:59.583 --> 00:47:02.857
You don't need to have fancy
gear or tools or anything

00:47:02.857 --> 00:47:03.725
to get started.

00:47:03.725 --> 00:47:08.009
You just start with what you
have, where you are, you start.

00:47:08.009 --> 00:47:09.793
You want to start a YouTube
channel, get out your phone

00:47:09.793 --> 00:47:10.952
and start making videos.

00:47:10.952 --> 00:47:13.231
Do you want to make huge productions?

00:47:13.231 --> 00:47:14.435
Yes, great.

00:47:14.435 --> 00:47:16.759
That's your longterm goal.

00:47:16.759 --> 00:47:18.915
So start working on it because
nobody's going to fund that

00:47:18.915 --> 00:47:21.488
for you, you have to bet on
yourself and start putting in

00:47:21.488 --> 00:47:24.024
the work to get good at it,
using the tools that you have.

00:47:24.024 --> 00:47:25.678
And so even just thinking about it,

00:47:25.678 --> 00:47:28.949
like one of the most important
skills of an entrepreneur

00:47:28.949 --> 00:47:31.771
is resourcefulness because you
never have enough resources

00:47:31.771 --> 00:47:33.013
to do what you want to do.

00:47:33.013 --> 00:47:34.797
So we have to be creative,
we have to be resourceful,

00:47:34.797 --> 00:47:37.284
that's how new things are invented.

00:47:37.284 --> 00:47:38.990
You can't just throw
money at a problem, right,

00:47:38.990 --> 00:47:40.326
you need resourcefulness.

00:47:40.326 --> 00:47:43.022
And so it's a great thing
for you to learn right now.

00:47:43.022 --> 00:47:45.343
And it's a real situation, right?

00:47:45.343 --> 00:47:47.996
You have bills, you have a family,

00:47:47.996 --> 00:47:50.610
you've got mouths to feed,
you don't have a ton of time.

00:47:50.610 --> 00:47:51.443
Great.

00:47:51.443 --> 00:47:53.157
Everybody's fighting their own battle.

00:47:53.157 --> 00:47:55.401
There's a lot of things that
I could say about my life

00:47:55.401 --> 00:47:57.480
or the reasons why I
can't build my business,

00:47:57.480 --> 00:47:59.596
just like everybody else.

00:47:59.596 --> 00:48:01.924
But I focus, I make time for it.

00:48:01.924 --> 00:48:04.993
At the start it was an hour
a day, half an hour a day,

00:48:04.993 --> 00:48:07.354
whatever it was, and you
start expanding as you start

00:48:07.354 --> 00:48:09.838
being able to provide
value and earn an income.

00:48:09.838 --> 00:48:13.077
And so just that mindset shift
of I'm not going to focus on

00:48:13.077 --> 00:48:15.025
what I don't have, here's what I do have,

00:48:15.025 --> 00:48:15.858
here's what I'm grateful for,

00:48:15.858 --> 00:48:18.433
here's how I'm going to maximize
what I do have right now.

00:48:18.433 --> 00:48:22.641
And how can I be more
resourceful to figure things out?

00:48:22.641 --> 00:48:24.209
Just switching that mindset
will make a huge, huge,

00:48:24.209 --> 00:48:26.538
huge difference, much more
than any specific tactic

00:48:26.538 --> 00:48:27.992
that I can give you.

00:48:27.992 --> 00:48:30.393
- Yeah, that's a great quote.

00:48:30.393 --> 00:48:34.038
Resourcefulness is always
the answer, not resources.

00:48:34.038 --> 00:48:36.106
And if you thinking of
starting your business,

00:48:36.106 --> 00:48:39.718
actually, it's a great
thing to do to moonlight.

00:48:39.718 --> 00:48:42.383
So, you know, you time
slice to what Evan said

00:48:42.383 --> 00:48:45.034
so you're working full-time,
you're funding, you know,

00:48:45.034 --> 00:48:48.107
the family, the mortgage,
the kids, and all that stuff.

00:48:48.107 --> 00:48:50.142
But maybe, when you put the
kids to bed, you actually

00:48:50.142 --> 00:48:52.790
sit down and think
about your business idea

00:48:52.790 --> 00:48:55.980
and use the evenings
to start your business

00:48:55.980 --> 00:48:58.715
and start your big idea and
how you're going to do that.

00:48:58.715 --> 00:49:01.285
Maybe carve some time out
on the weekends to do that.

00:49:01.285 --> 00:49:03.318
And then you start your
business and when it's kind of

00:49:03.318 --> 00:49:05.639
going up and it's really taking off,

00:49:05.639 --> 00:49:07.791
then you think about when do you step down

00:49:07.791 --> 00:49:09.411
from your full-time employment.

00:49:09.411 --> 00:49:11.896
But I know, again, loads
of great entrepreneurs

00:49:11.896 --> 00:49:16.876
who have, effectively,
moonlighted to kind of pump start

00:49:16.876 --> 00:49:19.870
their business and it's
been very effective.

00:49:19.870 --> 00:49:21.692
And the amazing thing
about entrepreneurs is just

00:49:21.692 --> 00:49:24.134
the resourcefulness
that people come up with

00:49:24.134 --> 00:49:26.993
in terms of the scarcest
resource we all have is time.

00:49:26.993 --> 00:49:31.228
Making it go as far as
it can to be successful.

00:49:31.228 --> 00:49:33.091
- One thing I always remind
myself of is no matter

00:49:33.091 --> 00:49:37.209
how bad a situation is,
remember somebody has it worse.

00:49:37.209 --> 00:49:39.032
Somebody's had it worse than you,

00:49:39.032 --> 00:49:41.824
had fewer resources than you
and went on to do amazing,

00:49:41.824 --> 00:49:43.319
amazing things.

00:49:43.319 --> 00:49:44.989
You know, if you think
of Oprah growing up,

00:49:44.989 --> 00:49:48.964
you know, being raped by family
members and being so poor

00:49:48.964 --> 00:49:53.480
that she had to wear potato
sacks to school as a dress.

00:49:53.480 --> 00:49:57.133
And just, it's story after
story after story after story

00:49:57.133 --> 00:50:00.779
of these ultra-successful
people who started with way less

00:50:00.779 --> 00:50:03.438
than what you already have
right now and the difference is

00:50:03.438 --> 00:50:06.756
they didn't focus on what
they don't have, right?

00:50:06.756 --> 00:50:09.743
Focus on what you do
have and be resourceful.

00:50:09.743 --> 00:50:13.434
- Yeah, actually, when I was
out in the Silicon Valley

00:50:13.434 --> 00:50:16.374
at the time, sort of late
'90s, loads of companies

00:50:16.374 --> 00:50:19.067
raising money and VC money.

00:50:19.067 --> 00:50:21.897
Typically the companies
that raised loads of money,

00:50:21.897 --> 00:50:24.295
not always successful.

00:50:24.295 --> 00:50:26.661
And also it destroyed
their culture because,

00:50:26.661 --> 00:50:29.445
to what Evan said, and I
think this is so fundamental,

00:50:29.445 --> 00:50:31.397
is that I wouldn't necessary
argue that you want to

00:50:31.397 --> 00:50:33.467
run a company on fumes,
but you want to run it

00:50:33.467 --> 00:50:34.300
lean and mean.

00:50:34.300 --> 00:50:37.442
Think that start-up, keep
that start-up mentality

00:50:37.442 --> 00:50:38.478
as you grow and scale.

00:50:38.478 --> 00:50:41.706
And there's some great papers
around founders' mentality,

00:50:41.706 --> 00:50:44.818
which keeps you fresh, keeps
you young, keeps you focused

00:50:44.818 --> 00:50:46.598
on customer obsession.

00:50:46.598 --> 00:50:49.957
And there's so many companies
I see who grow and grow

00:50:49.957 --> 00:50:53.728
and then they become wasteful
and they kind of lose that

00:50:53.728 --> 00:50:57.247
kind of wake up call of why
they started their adventure

00:50:57.247 --> 00:50:58.410
in the first place.

00:50:58.410 --> 00:51:00.568
So, again, you know, as
a start-up entrepreneur,

00:51:00.568 --> 00:51:03.138
I really think it's pretty
important if you really sort of

00:51:03.138 --> 00:51:05.997
almost run on fumes and you
haven't got loads of money

00:51:05.997 --> 00:51:08.276
to spend on marketing and stuff like that,

00:51:08.276 --> 00:51:11.975
you'll find ways through just
creativity and resourcefulness

00:51:11.975 --> 00:51:15.002
to get the job done, to meet
the end results, and to find

00:51:15.002 --> 00:51:17.824
some really efficient ways to do that.

00:51:17.824 --> 00:51:18.657
- I love it.

00:51:18.657 --> 00:51:19.935
I know we're starting
to run short on time.

00:51:19.935 --> 00:51:23.082
Steven's got a crazy schedule
here today at Sage Summit.

00:51:23.082 --> 00:51:23.959
One last question.

00:51:23.959 --> 00:51:27.778
Let's get to Cherique
who's saying does setting

00:51:27.778 --> 00:51:29.695
unrealistic goals work?

00:51:30.646 --> 00:51:31.730
- I'm realistic.

00:51:31.730 --> 00:51:33.889
I guess, if I reframe
that and said, you know,

00:51:33.889 --> 00:51:36.672
whether you call it big, hairy, audacious,

00:51:36.672 --> 00:51:39.031
I certainly think, well,
there's a couple of things.

00:51:39.031 --> 00:51:42.184
Goals are a result of plans, okay?

00:51:42.184 --> 00:51:44.422
One thing that's really
important and we probably should

00:51:44.422 --> 00:51:46.876
talk about this sometime, if
you do an incremental plan

00:51:46.876 --> 00:51:49.331
from where you are, you
can only see the next step.

00:51:49.331 --> 00:51:51.413
What you want to do as an entrepreneur

00:51:51.413 --> 00:51:53.233
is say the end game of, you know,

00:51:53.233 --> 00:51:54.604
what is it I want to dream?

00:51:54.604 --> 00:51:56.589
And you almost live in the future

00:51:56.589 --> 00:51:58.346
and then come back to the present.

00:51:58.346 --> 00:52:00.466
So, and this is the way most

00:52:00.466 --> 00:52:02.007
successful companies actually plan.

00:52:02.007 --> 00:52:03.121
They'll say, you know, three years,

00:52:03.121 --> 00:52:03.954
what do we wan to do?

00:52:03.954 --> 00:52:06.150
You know, we want to, say for Sage,

00:52:06.150 --> 00:52:08.844
launched a million
businesses, new businesses,

00:52:08.844 --> 00:52:10.750
help them get up and running.

00:52:10.750 --> 00:52:13.116
So if we did that then in three years,

00:52:13.116 --> 00:52:14.853
what would we have to do
in year and then kind of

00:52:14.853 --> 00:52:16.427
work it back to month one.

00:52:16.427 --> 00:52:19.621
And then I think it really
does give you an opportunity

00:52:19.621 --> 00:52:20.869
to have very ambitious plans.

00:52:20.869 --> 00:52:22.440
I think it's great to have ambitious plans

00:52:22.440 --> 00:52:24.259
and share those plans.

00:52:24.259 --> 00:52:26.751
And the more ambition you have, typically,

00:52:26.751 --> 00:52:28.211
the more successful you are.

00:52:28.211 --> 00:52:30.077
The other thing I'd say is just the way

00:52:30.077 --> 00:52:31.987
the human psyche works.

00:52:31.987 --> 00:52:36.872
If you say, I'll have a plan
where we'll have, I don't know,

00:52:36.872 --> 00:52:39.235
half a million dollars
of revenue in five years,

00:52:39.235 --> 00:52:40.600
or something like that.

00:52:40.600 --> 00:52:43.251
If you said, because the way it works is,

00:52:43.251 --> 00:52:46.648
then you'd be happy if
you maybe got $400,000,

00:52:46.648 --> 00:52:49.175
but if you said actually we
want to do a million dollars,

00:52:49.175 --> 00:52:52.619
it just drives your mentality
to be more ambitious

00:52:52.619 --> 00:52:53.816
and achieve more.

00:52:53.816 --> 00:52:57.468
So I would set yourself higher
goals and higher ambitions

00:52:57.468 --> 00:53:00.784
because, effectively, you'll
get probably 90% of that.

00:53:00.784 --> 00:53:04.186
And 90% of a double in
ambition level is much better

00:53:04.186 --> 00:53:07.584
than 100% of a very low ambition.

00:53:07.584 --> 00:53:09.831
- Yeah I think it's super
important to have a big goal

00:53:09.831 --> 00:53:10.863
that you're shooting towards,

00:53:10.863 --> 00:53:12.310
to have a reason to get up everyday.

00:53:12.310 --> 00:53:13.758
You know, I want to help
a billion entrepreneurs,

00:53:13.758 --> 00:53:16.536
that's inspiring, it leads me
to create a YouTube channel

00:53:16.536 --> 00:53:18.901
and do book deals and speak and do things

00:53:18.901 --> 00:53:21.434
that has the potential
to hit a lot of people

00:53:21.434 --> 00:53:25.731
At the same time, I think you
also need to enjoy the work.

00:53:25.731 --> 00:53:28.012
I'm not only doing this to hit this goal,

00:53:28.012 --> 00:53:30.332
whatever your big crazy
goal is, but I like grinding

00:53:30.332 --> 00:53:31.248
every single day.

00:53:31.248 --> 00:53:33.324
Like, I like do this, I
like editing my videos,

00:53:33.324 --> 00:53:35.648
I like talking to you one-on-one.

00:53:35.648 --> 00:53:39.043
So, I think too many people
just have a goal and it's like,

00:53:39.043 --> 00:53:41.781
okay, I'll do whatever it
takes just to hit that goal.

00:53:41.781 --> 00:53:43.067
I don't think that's the right mindset.

00:53:43.067 --> 00:53:44.722
I think you have to
actually enjoy the work

00:53:44.722 --> 00:53:46.710
that you're doing every
single day as well.

